// StdlibSource — what a compile knows about the STDLIB SOURCE it loaded, as opposed to the user's own
// code. Two facts, derived once per compile from one walk of the merged Maxon module:
//
//   1. WHICH STDLIB FUNCTIONS THIS PROGRAM CANNOT REACH — code dead-function elimination will drop
//      before the back end, so no pre-elimination pass may let it decide anything about the program.
//   2. WHICH STDLIB FUNCTIONS NEED THE x64-WINDOWS SUBSTRATE, and which runtime entry each one reaches —
//      the fact a user's own call site is refused with, so a target rejection names the stdlib function
//      THEY wrote instead of a line inside `stdlib/`.
//
// ⭐ **NOTHING HERE DEPENDS ON *WHICH* STDLIB FILES ARE LOADED — only on which functions came from
// stdlib.** shv2 loads a growing subset of `stdlib/` today and all of it eventually; both facts above get
// MORE important in that end state, not less, because a program built on `Map`, `String` and `FilePath`
// crosses into stdlib on nearly every line, and a diagnostic positioned inside one of them is a
// diagnostic about a file the user never opened.
//
// So the question asked throughout is **"is this function STDLIB source or USER source?"**, answered from
// the function's own source path (`isStdlibFunction`) — never from a registry of loaded modules. Grow the
// set of loaded files to every file under `stdlib/` and every function in this file still answers
// correctly, over a larger set, with no edit.

// ⭐ **WHICH FILES ARE STDLIB IS ESTABLISHED BY CONSTRUCTION, NOT BY RE-TESTING PATHS.** The stdlib loader
// enumerates `project.stdlibDir` and records every file it parsed from there in `project.stdlibSourcePaths`
// — so "this file lies under the checkout's `stdlib/`" is already known of exactly those files, and of no
// others. That record is what the facts below are derived from.
//
// ⚠ **THE ALTERNATIVE WAS MEASURED AND IS EXPENSIVE.** Re-deriving it by asking `FilePath.isInside` of
// each of the program's source paths costs about FOUR String allocations per question — on Windows
// `canonicalForCompare` clones and lower-cases both sides, and the needle is an interpolation — so it puts
// a term into every compile that grows with the number of files the user wrote. Measured on the scale
// ladder: +60 allocations at rung 0 rising to +1,920 at rung 5, doubling with the corpus, for a fact the
// loader already had. Asking it per FUNCTION instead of per file was worse again (+3,633 rising to
// +48,273). `isStdlibFunction` answers the per-function form from a set built once.
//
// ⭐ **IS THIS FUNCTION STDLIB SOURCE?** A `ByteArray` hash against that set. This is the question every
// pass asks, and it stays true as stdlib grows from one module to all of it — nothing here depends on
// WHICH files those are, only on their having come from `stdlib/`.
export function isStdlibFunction(project Project, func IrFunction) returns bool
	return project.stdlibFacts.stdlibFunctions.contains(func.name)
end 'isStdlibFunction'

// The directory name walked for, and a generous bound on how far up the tree to look. A checkout is
// shallow (`<root>/maxon-shv2/.maxon/maxon-shv2.exe` is three levels under the `stdlib/` that sits at
// `<root>`), so 64 is far more than enough while still terminating on a pathological path rather than
// looping forever.
//
// ⚠ **EXPORTED BECAUSE IT IS ALSO THE STDLIB'S MODULE DIRECTORY (N1c)** — `Queries.namespaceAnchorFor`
// anchors a stdlib file at this directory's PARENT, so every stdlib file's module directory is this name
// or a descendant of it. `Project.isStdlibModuleDirectory` is the one reader, and it reads this rather
// than a second `"stdlib"` literal so a rename cannot leave the two disagreeing about where the library
// layer is.
export let StdlibDirName = "stdlib"
let MaxStdlibSearchDepth = 64

// Locate the checkout's `stdlib/` by walking UP from the COMPILER's own executable directory.
//
// ⚠ NOT from `Directory.currentPath()` (v1's `findStdlibPath`): the spec runner and `run_program`
// compile a program in a throwaway temp dir, so the working directory has no `stdlib/` above it.
// The compiler binary always lives inside the checkout, so its own path is the reliable anchor —
// the same reason the bootstrap's `FindStdlibPath` (0-Compiler.cs) walks up from
// `AppContext.BaseDirectory`. A failure to locate it is a hard, LOUD error rather than a silent
// skip: every compile loads stdlib source, so a missing stdlib is a real environment problem the
// user must see, not a `Clock.nowMs()` that mysteriously reports E3004.
export function locateStdlibDir() returns FilePath throws CompileError
	let exePath = try Process.executablePath() otherwise throw CompileError.stdlibNotFound
	let exeDir = try exePath.parent() otherwise throw CompileError.stdlibNotFound
	return try stdlibDirAbove(exeDir)
end 'locateStdlibDir'

// The same walk, from a directory the CALLER names — which is a different question from the one above
// and has one other asker: `TreeLock` needs the checkout containing the path a command WRITES INTO
// (the project directory, the spec directory, the scale corpus), not the one the compiler's own
// sources came from. Those two answers are the same file in every ordinary invocation and are not
// required to be: the walk is what both of them mean by "the checkout", so it is written once.
//
// It THROWS rather than answering "none" for `locateStdlibDir`'s reason there; `TreeLock` reads the
// throw as "no checkout above this path", which is a real and ordinary answer for a program compiled
// in a temp directory.
export function stdlibDirAbove(start FilePath) returns FilePath throws CompileError
	var dir = start

	var depth = 0
	while depth < MaxStdlibSearchDepth 'search'
		// Interpolated so the argument is a committed `String`: a bare module-`let` literal stays
		// untyped and matches BOTH `FilePath.join` overloads (String / FilePath) — an E3007 ambiguity —
		// whereas a String VALUE selects `join(String)` cleanly.
		let candidate = dir.join("{StdlibDirName}")
		if Directory.exists(candidate) 'found'
			return candidate
		end 'found'
		// `parent()` throws at the filesystem root — the correct place for the search to give up.
		dir = try dir.parent() otherwise throw CompileError.stdlibNotFound
		depth = depth + 1
	end 'search'

	throw CompileError.stdlibNotFound
end 'stdlibDirAbove'

// A set of function names, for O(1) membership. Used for both "unreachable from `main`" and "declared in
// stdlib source", exactly like `DestructorSet`.
export typealias FunctionNameSet = Set with ByteArray

// ⭐⭐ **THE BODY DEMAND IS PER FILE AND HAS TWO STATES — all of this file's bodies, or none of them.**
// A user file always builds. A stdlib file builds once ANY function it declares is reached, and then it
// builds ALL of them.
//
// ⚠ **PER FILE RATHER THAN PER FUNCTION, AND BOTH REASONS ARE LOAD-BEARING:**
//
//   • **CORRECTNESS.** A per-function demand has to be asked in the parser, mid-declaration, of a name —
//     and there are TWO spellings of a function's name there. `sourceName` is what the source wrote
//     (`Math.exp`); `overloadRegistrationNameFor` may mangle it (`FilePath.join#FilePath`), and it is the
//     MANGLED one that reaches `IrFunction.name` and therefore the reachability walk's answer. Asking with
//     the wrong one skips a body the program calls, and the call then lands on the bare `retVoid`
//     `skipUnreachedFunctionBody` left — a SILENT WRONG ANSWER, in exactly the family
//     `deriveStdlibFacts` already warns about ("it runs BEFORE overload resolution, so a callee name here
//     is provisional"). A per-FILE demand never asks about a name at all.
//   • **COST.** The demand is discovered round by round, so a per-function demand re-parses a file once per
//     function of it that is reached — and, because a memo key built from the whole-program demand moves
//     for EVERY file whenever ANY name is added, it re-parses all twenty-one of them each round. MEASURED
//     before this was per-file: `Math.exp(0.0)` spent **125.9 ms in `parse`** against 54.2 ms for the
//     unmodified compiler — a REGRESSION on the programs the change exists to speed up.
//
// The cost of the coarser unit is building the unreached bodies of a file that is already being used, and
// `DeadFunctionElimination` drops those exactly as it dropped them before.
// (There is no typealias for it: `typealias X = bool` is not legal Maxon, and a one-word alias for a
// primitive would be a name to keep in step rather than a fact to state. The header above is the fact.)

// A stdlib function name -> the x64-windows-only runtime entry its body reaches, directly or through
// other stdlib functions. Membership IS "this function needs the Win32 substrate", so the map is its own
// mark column and there is no second set to keep in step with it.
export typealias StdlibSubstrateEntryMap = Map with (ByteArray, ByteArray)

// Stdlib function name -> its DENSE node index in the substrate fixpoint's call graph. A distinct alias
// from `FuncIndexByName`, whose values are merged-MODULE function indices: these index the far
// smaller stdlib-only key space `buildCsr` is handed.
typealias StdlibNodeByName = Map with (ByteArray, CsrIndex)

// ⭐ THE TWO FACTS ABOUT STDLIB SOURCE A COMPILE DERIVES, from ONE walk of the merged module. They are one
// object rather than two `Project` fields because they are one derivation over one call graph:
// `substrateEntries` is meaningful only for the functions `unreachable` does NOT hold (code the program
// cannot reach is invisible to it, substrate and all), and two fields assigned from two calls could come
// to disagree about which functions those are.
export type StdlibFacts
	// Every function the merged module got from stdlib source — the per-function form of `isStdlibPath`,
	// materialized once so the question can be asked of every function of the program without paying a
	// path comparison each time (see `isStdlibFunction`).
	export let stdlibFunctions as FunctionNameSet
	// Stdlib functions NOT reachable from `main`. Dead-function elimination drops them before the back
	// end, so a pass that runs earlier and examines function BODIES to decide something about the PROGRAM
	// — the runtime floor it carries (`scanRuntimeUsage`), the targets it can be built for
	// (`checkCalls`) — must not let them decide it, or the program pays for code it does not contain.
	// User functions are never in it: a diagnostic in unreached USER code is still the user's bug, while
	// a runtime requirement of unreached STDLIB code is nobody's.
	export let unreachable as FunctionNameSet
	// The REACHABLE stdlib functions that need the x64-windows substrate, and which runtime entry each
	// one reaches. Read at a call site that crosses from user code into stdlib, to refuse the program on
	// a target that cannot lower it — at the user's own span.
	export let substrateEntries as StdlibSubstrateEntryMap

	export static function create(stdlibFunctions FunctionNameSet, unreachable FunctionNameSet, substrateEntries StdlibSubstrateEntryMap) returns StdlibFacts
		return Self{stdlibFunctions: stdlibFunctions, unreachable: unreachable, substrateEntries: substrateEntries}
	end 'create'

	// A compile that has not derived them yet, and the answer for a compile that loaded no stdlib source
	// at all: no function is stdlib, nothing is unreachable-stdlib and nothing needs the substrate.
	export static function empty() returns StdlibFacts
		return Self.create(FunctionNameSet.create(), unreachable: FunctionNameSet.create(), substrateEntries: StdlibSubstrateEntryMap.create())
	end 'empty'
end 'StdlibFacts'

// ⭐ DERIVE BOTH FACTS. Runs once per compile, right after the module is merged and before any pass that
// reads either of them — and before `DeadGlobalElimination.liveGlobalLabels`, which is the earliest
// reader (`Compiler.compileToCodeResult`).
//
// ⭐ **IT TAKES `signatures` FOR ONE THING: THE FUNCTIONS A TOP-LEVEL INITIALIZER CALLS.** Those calls are
// made by `__module_init`, which does not exist yet at this point and is called by the entry STUB rather
// than from `main` once it does — so neither the walk below nor its short-circuit can see the edge in any
// body. It is read off the DECLARATION that causes the call instead (`ProgramSignatures.globalInitCallees`),
// which is also what lets this run before the synthesis: see that function for why the answer does not
// depend on the global liveness this derivation is about to decide.
export function deriveStdlibFacts(project Project, module MaxonModule, signatures ProgramSignatures) returns StdlibFacts throws CompileError
	// A prior diagnostic leaves the merged module and its registries describing a program that is already
	// refused, so deriving anything from it can only be noise — the refusal the four siblings around this
	// call already make (`checkConformance`, `checkWhereConstraints`, `checkTypeSymbolNamespace`,
	// `checkGenericAliasCycles`), and this was the one pre-pipeline derivation without it. Nothing that runs
	// before the pipeline's error gate READS these facts to report anything: `scanRuntimeUsage` only sets
	// usage bits, and the two passes that do report off them (`checkCalls`'s target refusal,
	// `insertRangeChecks`) are inside the pipeline, downstream of that gate.
	if projectHasErrors(project) 'malformedModule'
		return StdlibFacts.empty()
	end 'malformedModule'

	let stdlibNames = try stdlibFunctionNames(project)
	if stdlibNames.count() == 0 'noStdlibSource'
		return StdlibFacts.empty()
	end 'noStdlibSource'

	var stdlibSet = FunctionNameSet.create()
	for name in stdlibNames 'seed'
		stdlibSet.insert(name)
	end 'seed'

	// Built once, here, and handed to both walks below: the short-circuit and the precise walk ask
	// different questions of the same list, and a second build would be the same arena walked twice.
	let initCallees = signatures.globalInitCallees()

	// ⭐ COMMON-CASE SHORT-CIRCUIT. If no op in USER code REACHES stdlib, no stdlib function is
	// reachable at all, so ALL of them are unreachable and NONE can be blamed for a substrate — reached by
	// ONE allocation-free O(ops) scan, WITHOUT the O(functions) name->index map and reached-array the
	// from-`main` walk allocates, and without the substrate fixpoint. That map is the whole per-compile
	// memory cost of loading stdlib source into a program that never touches it, so skipping it keeps the
	// cost a per-compile CONSTANT instead of growing with the user program. It stays EXACT: a stdlib
	// function referenced by nothing is reachable from nothing.
	if not userCodeReachesStdlib(module, stdlib: stdlibSet, initCallees: initCallees) 'noneReferenced'
		var allUnreachable = FunctionNameSet.create()
		for name in stdlibNames 'markAll'
			allUnreachable.insert(name)
		end 'markAll'
		return StdlibFacts.create(stdlibSet, unreachable: allUnreachable, substrateEntries: StdlibSubstrateEntryMap.create())
	end 'noneReferenced'

	// User code DOES call stdlib — resolve exactly which stdlib functions the program REACHES from `main`
	// and mark only the untouched ones. This precise walk (and its maps) runs ONLY for a program that
	// actually uses stdlib.
	let reachable = try reachableMaxonFunctionNames(module, project: project, initCallees: initCallees)
	var unreachable = FunctionNameSet.create()
	for name in stdlibNames 'classify'
		if not reachable.contains(name) 'unreached'
			unreachable.insert(name)
		end 'unreached'
	end 'classify'

	return StdlibFacts.create(stdlibSet, unreachable: unreachable, substrateEntries: stdlibSubstrateEntries(module, stdlibNames: stdlibNames, unreachable: unreachable))
end 'deriveStdlibFacts'

// ⭐⭐ **GROW THE BODY DEMAND BY ONE ROUND — the fixpoint step `queryAllModule` iterates.**
//
// It asks the SAME question `deriveStdlibFacts` asks, of the same walk, and that is deliberate rather
// than convenient: the set of bodies this compile BUILDS and the set `lowerMaxonToStd` LOWERS must be
// decided by one derivation, or a body could be lowered that was never parsed. Round 0 merges every
// file with no stdlib body built, so the walk sees the user's own edges into stdlib and stops there;
// each later round has the bodies the previous one demanded, so the walk goes one call deeper. It
// terminates because the demand only ever grows and is bounded by the stdlib functions that exist.
//
// ⚠ **A ROUND THAT ADDS NOTHING IS THE COMMON CASE, AND IT IS THE FIRST ONE.** A program whose user
// code names no stdlib function at all — most of `specs-shv2` — settles here, having built not one
// stdlib body. That is exactly `userCodeReachesStdlib`'s short-circuit reached one tier earlier.
//
// Returns whether the demand actually grew, which is the caller's loop condition.
export function growStdlibBodyDemand(project Project, module MaxonModule, signatures ProgramSignatures) returns bool throws CompileError
	// A prior diagnostic leaves the merged module describing a program that is already refused, so a
	// reachability walk over it can only be noise — `deriveStdlibFacts` declines for the same reason, one
	// screen down, and a compile that stops here simply builds no further bodies.
	if projectHasErrors(project) 'malformedModule'
		return false
	end 'malformedModule'

	let stdlibNames = try stdlibFunctionNames(project)
	if stdlibNames.count() == 0 'noStdlibSource'
		return false
	end 'noStdlibSource'

	var stdlibSet = FunctionNameSet.create()
	for name in stdlibNames 'seed'
		stdlibSet.insert(name)
	end 'seed'

	let initCallees = signatures.globalInitCallees()
	// The same short-circuit `deriveStdlibFacts` opens with, and it is the whole saving: no op in code
	// this program reaches names a stdlib function, so no stdlib body can be reached and the walk's
	// O(functions) name->index map is never built.
	if not userCodeReachesStdlib(module, stdlib: stdlibSet, initCallees: initCallees) 'noneReferenced'
		return false
	end 'noneReferenced'

	let reachable = try reachableMaxonFunctionNames(module, project: project, initCallees: initCallees)

	// ⚠ **THE ANSWER IS RECORDED AS A SET OF FILES, NOT OF NAMES, AND THAT IS WHAT SURVIVES THE MEMO
	// DROP.** `queryAllModule` clears `parseCache` before it re-folds, so nothing may ask a later round
	// "which functions does this file declare?" — the artifact that knew is gone. A file recorded here is
	// still recorded then. (Measured the other way round: keyed on names and read back through the parse
	// cache, every file answered "declares nothing reached" after the drop, every body stayed skipped, and
	// `lowerStdToX64` panicked on the `retVoid` of a function the program calls.)
	var grew = false
	for path in project.stdlibSourcePaths 'eachFile'
		if project.db.demandedStdlibBodyFiles.contains(path) 'alreadyBuilding'
			continue
		end 'alreadyBuilding'

		let artifact = try queryParseOps(project, path: path)
		for func in artifact.module.functions 'eachFunc'
			if not reachable.contains(func.name) 'notReached'
				continue
			end 'notReached'

			project.db.demandedStdlibBodyFiles.insert(path)
			grew = true
			break
		end 'eachFunc'
	end 'eachFile'

	return grew
end 'growStdlibBodyDemand'

// Every function the merged module got from stdlib source, in load order. Read back from each stdlib
// file's PARSE ARTIFACT — a memo HIT, since `queryAllModule` has already parsed it — so the cost is
// O(stdlib functions) and touches nothing the user wrote. A `ByteArrayArray` rather than a set because
// the names are unique by construction (E3006 rejects a duplicate) and the substrate fixpoint needs a
// dense ORDER for its node space as well as membership.
function stdlibFunctionNames(project Project) returns ByteArrayArray throws CompileError
	var names = ByteArrayArray.create()
	for path in project.stdlibSourcePaths 'eachFile'
		let artifact = try queryParseOps(project, path: path)
		for func in artifact.module.functions 'eachFunc'
			names.push(func.name)
		end 'eachFunc'
	end 'eachFile'
	return names
end 'stdlibFunctionNames'

// The reachable-from-`main` closure over the merged Maxon module, as a set of function NAMES.
// The Maxon-tier twin of `DeadFunctionElimination.markCalleeOf`: the FOUR ops that name a callee
// are edges — `call`, `tryCall`, `functionRef` (a TAKEN ADDRESS is a static edge even though the
// call consuming it is indirect) and `asyncCall`. Every other variant names no static callee.
//
// A callee that is not a module function — an as-yet-uninstalled `__gt_*`/`__clock_*` runtime
// helper, which is added only during/after lowering — is simply not queued, exactly as
// `DeadFunctionElimination.markLiveFunction` handles the same case.
//
// ⛔⛔ **"REACHABILITY BY DIRECT `call`/`funcAddr` EDGES IS EXACT FOR STDLIB SOURCE ... BECAUSE NO STDLIB
// FUNCTION IS REACHED BY A WITNESS DISPATCH OR AN `.rdata` RELOC" IS WHAT THIS PARAGRAPH SAID, AND W35
// MADE IT FALSE.** That premise held only while `print` was a compiler-recognized BARE NAME rather than
// `stdlib/Print.maxon`'s declaration. The moment it became ordinary stdlib source, an interface method
// implementation that prints put a stdlib function behind a DATA edge — and
// `specs-shv2/interface-dispatch.md`'s `dispatch-void-method` (a `Thing.show()` whose body is
// `print("{label}\n")`, reached only through `__witness_Thing.Printer`) went from passing to a COMPILER
// PANIC: `requireUnreachableStdlibStayedDead: 'print' is in StdlibFacts.unreachable`. The guard was right
// and the walk was wrong, which is what a guard stating a premise is for.
//
// ⇒ **THE WITNESS EDGE IS NOW SEEDED AT THE OP THAT IMPLIES IT** — `markWitnessTableImpls`, the same shape
// `markCorpusCalleesOfSynthesizedRuntime` uses one screen down and for the same reason: a pre-pass over the
// module would fire on every program (every listed stdlib module declares conformances, so
// `witnessSlotImpls` is never empty), which is precisely the blanket-seed trap that comment measured at
// 2223 moved fragments.
//
// ⛔⛔ **WHAT IT STILL DOES NOT COVER, AND THE FIRST STATEMENT OF THIS WAS TOO NARROW — MEASURED AT THE
// W35 REVIEW.** It said the one uncovered shape was the constrained-generic witness and that "no corpus
// program does". Both halves needed correcting: **the uncovered shape is A WITNESS TABLE THAT IS
// MATERIALIZED WITHOUT ANY DISPATCH OP**, the trigger above is the dispatch op, and
// `LowerMaxonToStd.ensureWitnessTable` has TWO callers, NEITHER of which implies one.
//
//   • `materializeWitnessTableAddr` — an EXISTENTIAL WIDENING. A concrete conformer crossing into an
//     interface-typed parameter, field or return builds `__witness_<Type>.<Iface>` and relocates every
//     requirement slot; whether anything ever DISPATCHES through it is a separate question.
//     MEASURED at `396370e0f`: `type Thing implements Printer` whose `show()` body is `print(…)`,
//     boxed once into an interface-typed struct field and never dispatched, is
//     `panic … requireUnreachableStdlibStayedDead: 'print' is in StdlibFacts.unreachable`.
//   • `materializeWitnessArg` — a `where T is Iface` CONSTRAINT witness. Every instance method of a
//     constrained type carries the witness argument, so the table is built even for a method whose body
//     never touches `T`. MEASURED at the same commit, same panic, on a `type Box uses T where T is
//     Taker` whose only method returns a constant.
//
// ⚠ **BOTH PRE-DATE W35 AND NEITHER IS `print`-SPECIFIC — THE CONTROL WAS RUN.** Substituting `sleep(1)`
// for the `print(…)` in the first program panics identically on `'sleep'`, and `stdlib/Sleep.maxon` has
// been listed since long before this rung, whose changes touch nothing on that route. Since the seed
// above did not exist at all until W35, both programs panicked at the merge base too: what this rung
// changed is the EXPOSURE, not the hole — `print` is the most-called stdlib function there is, so the
// class went from "a witness impl that sleeps or reads the console" to "a witness impl that prints".
//
// ⇒ **CLOSING IT IS ITS OWN RUNG AND IS DELIBERATELY NOT ATTEMPTED HERE.** Both remaining triggers are
// facts about a CALLEE'S SIGNATURE (does it declare an interface-typed parameter; does it carry `where`
// constraints), not about an op, so covering them means a signature lookup per credited edge on this
// walk — and the seed is blanket, so any widening newly BUILDS stdlib bodies for a whole class of
// programs and moves goldens across the suite. That is a measured change, not a review edit.
// ⚠ And the seed's cost is filed with it: it re-walks every conformance × interface × transitive slot
// AT EVERY dispatch op, though the work is whole-program and idempotent after the first — O(dispatch
// ops × conformances × slots) where O(conformances × slots) would do. The scale corpus declares no
// interfaces, so no column of the ladder can see it.
//
// ⭐ The name index is the shared, CHECKED one (`FunctionNameIndex`, A1w). It used to resolve one
// last-wins and unchecked, walking ONE of two same-named bodies and crediting the callees of whichever it
// picked; a walk that silently under-approximates reachability is not something to leave resting on a
// tie-break nobody stated.
//
// ⚠ **THIS SITE NOW RUNS BEFORE `installManagedGlobalInit` (S2h), SO IT CAN NO LONGER MEET A REAL
// DUPLICATE** — a `stdlib/Builtins.maxon` declaration of `__module_init`/`__maxon_global_cleanup` against
// the compiler's own has no compiler side yet. That refusal did not move, it merely stopped being made
// TWICE: `PassPipeline.run`'s index sees the identical pair one screen below, and
// `DeadFunctionElimination.markCompilerOwnedRoot` catches the case with no pair to count. See
// `FunctionNameIndex`, which is where what each site can see is written down.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE ROOT SET IS `main` PLUS EVERY FUNCTION A TOP-LEVEL INITIALIZER CALLS, and the second half is
// not a widening — it is the same reachability with an edge no BODY carries.** `__module_init` makes those
// calls, and it is synthesized after this walk and called by the entry STUB, so it is invisible here in
// both directions. `DeadFunctionElimination.seedRoots` roots it unconditionally (its kind 2), so leaving
// the edge out is not conservative: the two derivations disagree, `lowerMaxonToStd` lowers no body for a
// stdlib factory named only by an initializer, and the elimination walk reaches the empty function it
// left. MEASURED before the fix, on `var sep = FilePath.separator()`: a `requireUnreachableStdlibStayedDead`
// panic on a program with nothing wrong with it (`specs-shv2/top-level-factory-globals.md`'s
// `stdlib-factory-reached-from-the-initializer`).
//
// ⚠⚠ **AND IT RUNS BEFORE OVERLOAD RESOLUTION, SO A CALLEE NAME HERE IS PROVISIONAL — WHICH IS WHY THE
// OVERLOAD SET IS CREDITED WHOLE.** `SemanticCheck.resolveOverloadedCalls` rewrites `call FilePath.join`
// into `call FilePath.join#FilePath` — and its own header opens *"OVERLOAD RESOLUTION RUNS FIRST, BEFORE
// ANYTHING READS A CALLEE"* — but it runs inside `PassPipeline.run`, and `deriveStdlibFacts` runs one
// screen ABOVE that call in `compileToCodeResult`. So this walk is the one reader that precedes it, and
// the bare name it sees is the registration name of the FIRST member only.
//
// MEASURED, and it is a compiler PANIC on a correct program, not a size regression:
// `specs/filepath.md`'s `filepath-join-filepath` writes `base.join(child)` against
// `stdlib/FilePath.maxon:357/371`'s two `join` overloads. The walk credited `FilePath.join` (the `String`
// member, declared first), filed `FilePath.join#FilePath` as unreachable, `lowerMaxonToStd` skipped its
// body — and then dead-function elimination, which runs AFTER resolution and sees the real callee,
// reached it and tripped `requireUnreachableStdlibStayedDead`.
//
// ⇒ A provisional callee names a SET, and this walk answers "might this be reached?", so every member is
// credited. That is exact for the question asked and cannot make the assert fire: an OVER-approximation
// only ever removes names from `unreachable`, and every consumer of that set (`lowerMaxonToStd`,
// `insertRangeChecks`, `scanRuntimeUsage`, `checkCalls`) treats absence as "compile it normally".
// ⚠ Moving `resolveOverloadedCalls` above this instead was considered and REJECTED: it sits after
// `resolveTypes` today, so hoisting it would hand overload selection the UNRESOLVED parameter spellings
// and could change which member a call picks — trading a loud panic for a silent misdispatch.
//
// The unused member is still dropped from the emitted image: dead-function elimination runs after
// resolution, keys on the real callee, and prunes it there. What this costs is only that
// `scanRuntimeUsage` may read an unreached sibling overload's body — and only in a program that already
// calls that stdlib name.
function reachableMaxonFunctionNames(module MaxonModule, project Project, initCallees ByteArrayArray) returns FunctionNameSet throws CompileError
	let byName = (try FunctionNameIndex.create(module.functions, project: project)).byName

	var reached = BoolArray.create()
	reached.resize(module.funcCount())
	var worklist = FuncIndexArray.create()
	var names = FunctionNameSet.create()

	markReachable(UserEntryName.toByteArray(), project: project, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
	for callee in initCallees 'eachInitializerRoot'
		markReachable(callee, project: project, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
	end 'eachInitializerRoot'

	while worklist.count() > 0 'walk'
		let idx = try worklist.pop() otherwise panic("reachableMaxonFunctionNames: pop from a non-empty worklist")
		let func = module.getFunc(idx)
		markConformerWitnessImpls(func, project: project, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
		markMaxonCallees(module, project: project, func: func, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
	end 'walk'

	return names
end 'reachableMaxonFunctionNames'

// Mark `name` reachable and queue its body. A name no module function carries (a runtime helper,
// or a root whose runtime this program never installs) is not found and not queued — nothing to
// walk. Mirrors `DeadFunctionElimination.markLiveFunction`.
function markReachable(name ByteArray, project Project, byName FuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray, names FunctionNameSet)
	markOneReachable(name, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)

	// The provisional-callee rule (see this walk's header): a name that is an overload SET's base names
	// every member, because which one the call means is not decided until `resolveOverloadedCalls`. The
	// bare name marked above is the FIRST member's registration name and is already covered; the rest are
	// these.
	//
	// ⚠ **`contains` BEFORE `get`, AND THE HONEST STATUS OF THAT IS: NOT MEASURED TO MATTER HERE.** The
	// reasoning is that this runs once per CALL EDGE and a miss is what nearly every edge is, so a
	// `try map.get(k) otherwise return` would be an error construction per edge on the common path. That
	// reasoning is why it is spelled this way. **It is NOT why the ladder moved**: it was this rung's first
	// hypothesis for a measured +0.101 allocations per source byte, it was built, and re-running the ladder
	// showed **not one allocation changed at any of the six rungs** — the real term was
	// `SemanticCheck.resolveOverloadedCalls` (see `maxonOpCalleeKind`). Kept because a `contains`/`get` pair
	// cannot be worse than a throwing `get` and it lets the miss be a `panic` rather than a silent `return`;
	// recorded this way because a comment claiming a measurement its own instrument refuted is exactly the
	// false prose this project keeps finding.
	if not project.overloadSets.contains(name) 'notAnOverloadSet'
		return
	end 'notAnOverloadSet'

	let members = try project.overloadSets.get(name) otherwise panic("markReachable: overloadSets lost the key '{String.from(name)}' between `contains` and `get`")
	for member in members 'eachMember'
		markOneReachable(member, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
	end 'eachMember'
end 'markReachable'

// Mark exactly ONE function name, with no overload widening — the body `markReachable` shares between the
// name it was handed and each member of that name's overload set. Split out so the widening is written
// once and cannot recurse into itself (a member's own registration name keys no set).
function markOneReachable(name ByteArray, byName FuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray, names FunctionNameSet)
	let idx = try byName.get(name) otherwise 'notAModuleFunction'
		return
	end 'notAModuleFunction'

	let already = try reached.get(idx) otherwise panic("reachableMaxonFunctionNames: reached.get OOB at function index {idx}")
	if already 'alreadyReached'
		return
	end 'alreadyReached'

	try reached.set(idx, value: true) otherwise panic("reachableMaxonFunctionNames: reached.set OOB at function index {idx}")
	names.insert(name)
	worklist.push(idx)
end 'markOneReachable'

// Walk `block.opRefs` DIRECTLY — not `IrModule.liveOpIndices`, which allocates a fresh op-index array
// per function. This runs at the Maxon tier BEFORE any block-rebuilding pass, so `opRefs` holds no
// orphan ops (the concern that forces `liveOpIndices` at the Std tier), which is exactly why
// `scanRuntimeUsage` walks the same way here. A terminator names no callee, so visiting only the body
// ops in `opRefs` is complete for reachability.
function markMaxonCallees(module MaxonModule, project Project, func IrFunction, byName FuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray, names FunctionNameSet)
	for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
		let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("markMaxonCallees: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")
		for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
			let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("markMaxonCallees: opRef {opIdx} not in module.ops")
			markMaxonCalleeEdge(op, project: project, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
			markCorpusCalleesOfSynthesizedRuntime(op, project: project, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
		end 'eachOp'
	end 'eachBlock'
end 'markMaxonCallees'

// The static reachability edge one `MaxonOp` names, if any — asked of the DIALECT'S OWN classifier, which
// is the single place the op set answers "does this variant bind a callee name, and which operand is it?".
//
// ⚠⚠ **THIS USED TO BE A THIRD COPY OF THAT TABLE, AND ITS STATED REASON DIED IN THE SAME COMMIT THAT
// LEFT IT STANDING** (BATCH2 slice 3 review). It re-derived the classification with its own band ranges,
// justified by `maxonOpCallee` allocating a union box per ask — a cost this slice's own optimizer pass
// removed by making the classifier a SCALAR enum (`maxonOpCalleeKind`). With the cost gone the copy is
// only a second answer: a variant appended to `MaxonOp` does still fail to compile at both sites (the
// band-append invariant), but nothing makes the two sites classify it the SAME WAY, and an
// under-approximation here is not a compile error — it is `lowerMaxonToStd` skipping a body that is then
// reached, which is exactly the `requireUnreachableStdlibStayedDead` panic this slice was opened by.
//
// A `functionValue` target is credited alongside a `direct` callee: a `functionRef`'s target is reachable
// code even though no `call` op names it. `indirectCall` calls through the code POINTER that `functionRef`
// already credited, and `maxonOpCalleeKind` answers `noCallee` for it.
function markMaxonCalleeEdge(op MaxonOp, project Project, byName FuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray, names FunctionNameSet)
	match maxonOpCalleeKind(op) 'kind'
		direct to functionValue then markReachable(maxonOpCalleeName(op), project: project, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
		noCallee then break 'kind'
	end 'kind'
end 'markMaxonCalleeEdge'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE DATA EDGE THIS WALK CANNOT SEE — A WITNESS TABLE'S METHOD SLOTS — CREDITED AT THE CONFORMER
// THE WALK REACHES (W35).** `__witness_<Type>.<Iface>`'s slots are `.rdata` relocations, so the
// implementation each one names is named by no `call` anywhere in the module. Without this, an interface
// method whose body calls a stdlib function leaves that function filed `unreachable` while
// `DeadFunctionElimination` — which DOES follow those relocations — reaches it, and the two derivations
// disagree exactly as `requireUnreachableStdlibStayedDead` says.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE KEY IS THE CONFORMER, AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES IT NARROW.** `LowerMaxonToStd.ensureWitnessTable`
// has THREE callers and none of them is one op shape: an existential WIDENING at a call argument
// (`appendExistentialWitnessArgs`) or at a RETURN (`witnessForExistentialValue`), and a `where T is Iface`
// CONSTRAINT witness (`materializeWitnessArg`). Rather than predict all three from signatures, this asks the
// question they all share — **a witness table for `T` can only be built if a `T` VALUE EXISTS, and a `T`
// value can only be produced by a function OF `T`.** A type cannot be constructed from outside its own
// methods (E3076), so reaching some `T.<method>` is a precondition for every one of the three, and it is a
// precondition this walk already computes.
//
// ⇒ Reaching any function of a conforming type credits THAT type's requirement impls, and no others.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE FIRST TWO CUTS WERE BOTH WRONG, IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS, AND BOTH ARE WORTH KEEPING HERE.**
//   • **TOO NARROW:** keyed on a `witnessDispatch` OP. It fixed the dispatched case and left the other two
//     `ensureWitnessTable` callers panicking — a `Thing` boxed into an interface-typed field and never
//     dispatched, and a `where T is Digest` method that never touches `T`. Both compile on the merge base,
//     so the reachable-program set REGRESSED.
//   • **TOO BROAD:** keyed on a signature predicate, crediting EVERY conformance in the program. MEASURED
//     on one interface-boxing program, same binary both ways: allocations **370,345 ⇒ 671,836 (+81%)**,
//     bytes +20.9 MB, and the emitted code moved **3,388 ⇒ 3,788** — because `project.conformances` holds
//     every LISTED STDLIB module's conformances too, so `URL.equals`/`FilePath.hash` and their whole files'
//     bodies were demanded by a program that names neither. That is the blanket-seed trap
//     `markCorpusCalleesOfSynthesizedRuntime` measured at 2223 moved fragments, arriving by a second road.
//   ⇒ The conformer key is what makes it exact: a program that never reaches `URL` credits nothing of it.
//
// ⚠ **IT JOINS `<Type>.<requirement>` RATHER THAN READING `project.witnessSlotImpls`, AND A CUT THAT READ
// THAT MAP WAS WRONG. MEASURED:** the map is filled by `checkConformance`, which runs AFTER `queryAllModule`
// — and `growStdlibBodyDemand`'s fixpoint, which decides which stdlib bodies are BUILT AT ALL, runs INSIDE
// it. So the map is empty for every round of the fixpoint and full only by the time `deriveStdlibFacts`
// asks: the panic went away (the facts were right) and the emitted `@print` was an EMPTY function the
// program called — a silent wrong answer in place of a loud one, which is strictly worse.
// `conformances`/`interfaces` are merge-time stores (`Queries.resetMergeTargets` resets all three
// together), so they answer the same in both askers, which is the property this walk's header demands.
// ⇒ The `<Type>.<method>` join `ensureWitnessTable` is forbidden to make (R10) is correct HERE for the
// reason it is wrong there: a name no module function carries is silently skipped by `markReachable`, so a
// join that names nothing costs nothing, where a RELOCATION that names nothing is a wrong code pointer.
// Overloads are covered because `markReachable` credits the whole set.
//
// ⚠ **THE FOURTH `ensureWitnessTable` ROUTE — `withWitnessTableArgs` / `appendArrayElementWitnessArgs`, the
// `Hashable`/`Equatable` witness a `Set`/`Map`/`Array` threads — NEEDS NOTHING EXTRA TODAY, AND THAT IS
// MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED.** Its conformer is the container's KEY or ELEMENT, and a USER type there is
// refused: *"E2015: `Set` hashes and compares its keys through their `Hashable`/`Equatable` witnesses, so a
// key must be one of `int`, `String`, `Character`, `Array` — a 'Point' key is a later slice"*. So every such
// conformer is a BUILTIN whose impls `BuiltinConformanceRuntime` SYNTHESIZES, and a synthesized body calls no
// stdlib source, so nothing behind that table can be filed unreachable. The day user-type keys land, the
// conformer key above already covers them — reaching `Point.create` to put one in a `Set` credits
// `Point.hash` — which is a third reason to key on the conformer rather than on the three call sites.
function markConformerWitnessImpls(func IrFunction, project Project, byName FuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray, names FunctionNameSet)
	match splitQualifiedName(func.name) 'owner'
		// A FREE function belongs to no type, so reaching it produces no conformer and implies no table.
		notQualified then break 'owner'
		parts(typeName, _) then markWitnessImplsOfConformer(typeName, project: project, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
	end 'owner'
end 'markConformerWitnessImpls'

// Credit every requirement implementation `typeName`'s witness tables can name. A type that declares no
// conformance has no table and nothing to credit, which is almost every type in almost every program.
//
// ⚠ The index is `declaredConformanceIndex`'s, not a second walk of `project.conformances`: that memo is
// reset in lockstep with the store it derives from (`Queries.resetMergeTargets`), so it cannot answer this
// walk from a previous merge round's declarations.
function markWitnessImplsOfConformer(typeName ByteArray, project Project, byName FuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray, names FunctionNameSet)
	let conformedTo = try declaredConformanceIndex(project).get(typeName) otherwise 'declaresNoConformance'
		return
	end 'declaresNoConformance'

	for interfaceName in conformedTo 'eachInterface'
		let iface = try project.interfaces.get(interfaceName) otherwise 'noSuchInterface'
			// A conformance naming an interface the program does not declare is E3017's, reported by
			// `checkConformance` one pass later; a reachability walk has nothing to add and nothing to credit.
			continue
		end 'noSuchInterface'

		// The TRANSITIVE requirement set, `ensureWitnessTable`'s own list — `iface.methods` alone would miss
		// every `extends`-inherited slot, which is a slot like any other.
		for slot in interfaceWitnessSlots(project.interfaces, iface: iface) 'eachSlot'
			markReachable(qualifiedMethodName(typeName, methodName: slot.method.name), project: project, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
		end 'eachSlot'
	end 'eachInterface'
end 'markWitnessImplsOfConformer'

// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE CALL EDGE THIS WALK CANNOT SEE, SEEDED AT THE OP THAT IMPLIES IT.**
// `__str_trim`, `__cs_contains`, the `__strix_*` walks and the two decoding views are SYNTHESIZED Std
// bodies, appended after the pipeline (`Compiler.compile`'s install block). Their calls into
// `stdlib/helpers/string/` are therefore not edges of the MAXON call graph — the caller does not exist yet
// when this walk runs — so without this the corpus segmenter would be filed `unreachable`,
// `lowerMaxonToStd` would lower no body for it, and the installed body would call a symbol nothing defined.
//
// ⭐ **IT IS A SEED AND NOT A SECOND REACHABILITY RULE.** Everything BELOW these four names is found by the
// ordinary walk: `findGraphemeEnd` calls `utf8DecodeAt`, `graphemeBreakProperty`, `isGraphemeBreak` and the
// state helpers, and every one of those is an honest Maxon edge. Only the ENTRY points are declared.
//
// ⚠⚠ **IT RUNS INSIDE THE WALK, PER REACHED OP, AND A PRE-PASS OVER THE MODULE WAS MEASURABLY WRONG.**
// The first version scanned every function of the merged module up front — which includes STDLIB's OWN
// bodies, and a listed module calls `.codepoints()`. So the seed fired on EVERY program: `return 1 + 1`
// lowered the whole corpus segmenter, registered its eleven panic literals in `.rdata`, and shifted
// `__str_blob_0` to `__str_blob_11` in **2223 committed fragments** of programs containing no string at
// all. (DFE then dropped the code, so it was numbering and lowering work, never emitted bytes — which is
// exactly why a golden count was the only thing that could see it.) This is the trap `userCodeReachesStdlib`
// documents one screen down — *"stdlib is internally connected, so scanning its bodies would find an
// intra-stdlib edge on EVERY compile"* — and skipping stdlib bodies would NOT have been the fix either: a
// REACHED stdlib body that calls one of these entries needs the seed just as much as a user body does.
// Asking at the op, inside the walk, is exact for both: the question becomes "does something this program
// actually reaches name one of these?", which is the question the walk exists to answer.
function markCorpusCalleesOfSynthesizedRuntime(op MaxonOp, project Project, byName FuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray, names FunctionNameSet)
	if not opNamesGraphemeRuntimeEntry(op) 'notASynthesizedSegmenterCaller'
		return
	end 'notASynthesizedSegmenterCaller'

	markReachable(CorpusFindGraphemeEnd, project: project, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
	markReachable(CorpusCountGraphemes, project: project, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
	markReachable(CorpusUtf8DecodeAt, project: project, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
	markReachable(CorpusUtf8ByteLengthAt, project: project, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
end 'markCorpusCalleesOfSynthesizedRuntime'

// One op. Asks the callee question through `maxonOpCalleeKind`/`maxonOpCalleeName` — the same pair
// `opNamesStdlibCallee` reads — rather than matching the call forms here, so a new call form is learned by
// both scans at once.
function opNamesGraphemeRuntimeEntry(op MaxonOp) returns bool
	return match maxonOpCalleeKind(op) 'kind'
		direct to functionValue gives calleeIsGraphemeRuntimeEntry(maxonOpCalleeName(op))
		noCallee gives false
	end 'kind'
end 'opNamesGraphemeRuntimeEntry'

// ⚠ **`isStringIndexRuntimeCallee` IS READ, NOT TRANSCRIBED.** That predicate is `StringIndexRuntime`'s own
// answer to "is this one of my entry points", already a prefix test for the reason a roster would be wrong;
// spelling `__strix_` a second time here would be this project's signature bug in the file that seeds it.
function calleeIsGraphemeRuntimeEntry(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	return callee.equals(StrTrimName) or callee.equals(CharSetContainsName) or callee.equals(StrToCodepointsName) or callee.equals(StrToUtf16Name) or isStringIndexRuntimeCallee(callee)
end 'calleeIsGraphemeRuntimeEntry'

// True iff any live op in USER code names a STDLIB callee, or a TOP-LEVEL INITIALIZER does — the
// allocation-free pre-scan that lets a program which never touches stdlib skip the reachability walk and
// the substrate fixpoint entirely. It asks the same dialect classifier `markMaxonCalleeEdge` does
// (`maxonOpCalleeKind`), and differs from it only in what it does with a hit: it runs on every compile, so
// it must allocate nothing (no map, no queue) — it only asks "is one present?".
//
// ⚠ **IT ANSWERS OVER THE SAME `initCallees` LIST THE PRECISE WALK ROOTS FROM — that shared list is the
// guarantee, not a matching root SET**, and the difference is worth stating because the two are easy to
// confuse. `markReachable` widens a root over `project.overloadSets` and seeds the grapheme entries per
// reached op; this asks only "is this bare name a stdlib name?". Those agree EXACTLY, and here is why: a
// USER callee's own body is scanned by the loop below whatever it goes on to call, a STDLIB callee's bare
// name is the first overload member's registration name and so is always in `stdlib`, and an undeclared one
// is refused by the evaluator before it can reach either walk. An under-answer here would file ALL of
// stdlib unreachable for a program that reaches it — the direction that empties a body the back end then
// calls — so a new initializer form owes that argument again rather than inheriting it.
function userCodeReachesStdlib(module MaxonModule, stdlib FunctionNameSet, initCallees ByteArrayArray) returns bool
	// ⭐ **A TOP-LEVEL INITIALIZER IS A CROSSING NO BODY CARRIES.** `var sep = FilePath.separator()` reaches
	// stdlib without any function of this module naming it — the call belongs to `__module_init`, which does
	// not exist yet. The precise walk below roots exactly these, so missing one here would answer "all of
	// stdlib is unreachable" for a program that reaches it, which is the direction that skips a body the
	// back end then calls.
	for callee in initCallees 'eachInitializerRoot'
		if stdlib.contains(callee) 'found'
			return true
		end 'found'
	end 'eachInitializerRoot'

	for func in module.functions 'eachFunc'
		// ⭐ SKIP STDLIB's OWN BODIES. Stdlib is internally connected (`Clock.elapsedMs` calls
		// `Clock.nowMs`), so scanning its bodies would find an intra-stdlib edge on EVERY compile and
		// defeat this short-circuit. The exact question is "does USER code cross INTO stdlib?", and the
		// first edge into stdlib on any path from `main` is made by a USER caller — `main` itself is never
		// stdlib — so scanning only user bodies is the exact condition. (A stdlib-only reference is
		// unreachable-from-`main` anyway, so a false negative here is impossible; a false positive would
		// merely trigger the exact full walk.)
		//
		// It reads the set being BUILT rather than `isStdlibFunction`, which reads the PUBLISHED one:
		// this runs inside the derivation, before `project.stdlibFacts` holds anything.
		if stdlib.contains(func.name) 'skipStdlibBody'
			continue
		end 'skipStdlibBody'

		// Direct `block.opRefs` walk — allocation-free, and safe for the same reason `markMaxonCallees`
		// and `scanRuntimeUsage` use it at this tier (pre-pipeline: no orphan ops; callees are body ops).
		for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
			let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("userCodeReachesStdlib: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")
			for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
				let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("userCodeReachesStdlib: opRef {opIdx} not in module.ops")
				if opNamesStdlibCallee(op, stdlib: stdlib) or opNamesGraphemeRuntimeEntry(op) 'found'
					return true
				end 'found'
			end 'eachOp'
		end 'eachBlock'
	end 'eachFunc'
	return false
end 'userCodeReachesStdlib'

// Whether one `MaxonOp` names a callee in `stdlib` — the SAME dialect classifier `markMaxonCalleeEdge`
// asks, over the same four callee-bearing variants.
//
// ⚠ Its header used to say it was *"a SEPARATE band match on purpose: it runs on every compile, so it
// must allocate nothing (no boxed callee union…)"*. That was true of `maxonOpCallee`; it is not true of
// `maxonOpCalleeKind`, which is a scalar enum and allocates nothing, and the NAME is fetched only for the
// two kinds that carry one — i.e. only where this function was going to read a callee anyway. The
// allocation-free property the comment defended is kept, and the second copy that was defending it is not.
function opNamesStdlibCallee(op MaxonOp, stdlib FunctionNameSet) returns bool
	return match maxonOpCalleeKind(op) 'kind'
		direct to functionValue gives stdlib.contains(maxonOpCalleeName(op))
		noCallee gives false
	end 'kind'
end 'opNamesStdlibCallee'

// ⭐ **WHICH STDLIB FUNCTIONS NEED THE x64-WINDOWS SUBSTRATE, AND WHICH RUNTIME ENTRY EACH REACHES.**
// The fact a user's crossing call is blamed with, so the refusal names the stdlib function they wrote
// instead of a line inside `stdlib/`.
//
// ⭐ TRANSITIVE, because a stdlib module is written in LAYERS. `Clock.elapsedMs` names no runtime entry at
// all — it calls `Clock.nowMs`, which names `__gt_now_ns` — and a program calling `elapsedMs` must be
// refused at ITS call. So the requirement propagates BACKWARD along the stdlib call graph to a fixpoint:
// the seeds are the functions that name an entry directly, and a caller inherits its callee's.
//
// ⚠ **IT CARRIES THE ENTRY NAME, NOT A BIT**, which is why it is not the parser's `reverseReachabilityMark`:
// `Clock` alone reaches TWO entries (`__gt_now_ns` for the monotonic clock, `__clock_now_unix_s` for the
// calendar), so a bit would leave the diagnostic naming whichever one it guessed — a wrong answer, not a
// cosmetic one. What PROPAGATES is still a scalar: a node inherits its callee's SEED INDEX, and the names
// are read off `seedEntries` once at the end.
//
// COST: O(stdlib functions + their ops + their intra-stdlib edges), bounded by the stdlib source LOADED
// and not by the user program, and computed only for a compile that actually calls into stdlib. Each node
// enters the worklist at most once and each reverse edge is walked once, so it stays linear in the loaded
// stdlib as that grows from today's one module to all of it.
//
// The UNREACHABLE stdlib functions are left out entirely: they are invisible to this compile, so their
// substrate is too. That is what keeps a `Clock.nowMs()` in code `main` never calls compiling for wasm.
function stdlibSubstrateEntries(module MaxonModule, stdlibNames ByteArrayArray, unreachable FunctionNameSet) returns StdlibSubstrateEntryMap
	let nodeCount = stdlibNames.count()
	var nodeOf = StdlibNodeByName.create()
	for i in 0 upto nodeCount 'index'
		nodeOf.upsert(try stdlibNames.get(i) otherwise panic("stdlibSubstrateEntries: stdlibNames.get({i}) OOB below its own count()"), value: i)
	end 'index'

	var state = SubstrateFixpoint.overNodes(nodeCount)
	for func in module.functions 'eachFunc'
		if unreachable.contains(func.name) 'notInTheProgram'
			continue
		end 'notInTheProgram'
		if let node = try nodeOf.get(func.name) 'stdlibFunction'
			for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
				let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("stdlibSubstrateEntries: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")
				for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
					let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("stdlibSubstrateEntries: opRef {opIdx} not in module.ops")
					noteSubstrateEdge(op, callerNode: node, nodeOf: nodeOf, state: state)
				end 'eachOp'
			end 'eachBlock'
		end 'stdlibFunction'
	end 'eachFunc'

	state.propagateToCallers()

	var entries = StdlibSubstrateEntryMap.create()
	for i in 0 upto nodeCount 'publish'
		if state.requiresSubstrate(i) 'needsIt'
			entries.upsert(try stdlibNames.get(i) otherwise panic("stdlibSubstrateEntries: stdlibNames.get({i}) OOB in publish"), value: state.entryOf(i))
		end 'needsIt'
	end 'publish'
	return entries
end 'stdlibSubstrateEntries'

// The mutable state of the substrate fixpoint over the stdlib call graph's DENSE node space. It is one
// object rather than six threaded columns because the body scan and the propagation write all of it, and a
// helper that took them apart could be handed five of the six.
//
// ⭐ THE COLUMNS PROPAGATE SCALARS. A marked node stores an INDEX into `seedEntries`, never the entry name
// itself: reading a name out of the answer while inserting into it is a read-borrow held across a mutation,
// which the ownership checker refuses (E3070) — correctly, since the insert may reallocate what the read
// borrowed. The names are read once, after the fixpoint has settled.
type SubstrateFixpoint
	// Per node: does it (transitively) need the Win32 substrate? Also the "already queued" test — a node is
	// marked exactly when it enters the worklist, so it enters at most once.
	var marked as BoolArray
	// Per MARKED node: the index into `seedEntries` of the seed it inherits its entry from.
	var seedOf as CsrColumn
	// The runtime entry each seed names, in the order the seeds were found.
	var seedEntries as ByteArrayArray
	// Nodes whose callers still have to inherit from them. Drained by index, never popped, so a node's
	// position is stable while the array grows.
	var worklist as CsrColumn
	// The intra-stdlib call graph, REVERSED as it is collected (callee -> caller), so the propagation
	// walks from a function that names an entry to the functions that reach it.
	var edgeCallee as CsrColumn
	var edgeCaller as CsrColumn

	static function overNodes(nodeCount CsrIndex) returns SubstrateFixpoint
		var marked = BoolArray.create()
		marked.resize(nodeCount)
		var seedOf = CsrColumn.create()
		seedOf.resize(nodeCount)
		return Self{marked: marked, seedOf: seedOf, seedEntries: ByteArrayArray.create(), worklist: CsrColumn.create(), edgeCallee: CsrColumn.create(), edgeCaller: CsrColumn.create()}
	end 'overNodes'

	// `node` names `entry` directly — a SEED. The FIRST entry a body names is the one it is reported for: a
	// body naming two is refused for either, and both are equally true of it.
	function seed(node CsrIndex, entry ByteArray)
		let already = try self.marked.get(node) otherwise panic("SubstrateFixpoint.seed: marked.get({node}) outside the node space")
		if already 'alreadySeeded'
			return
		end 'alreadySeeded'
		self.mark(node, seedIndex: self.seedEntries.count())
		self.seedEntries.push(entry)
	end 'seed'

	// A call edge inside stdlib, recorded reversed for the propagation below.
	function callEdge(calleeNode CsrIndex, callerNode CsrIndex)
		self.edgeCallee.push(calleeNode)
		self.edgeCaller.push(callerNode)
	end 'callEdge'

	// Mark `node` as needing the substrate `seedIndex` names, and queue its callers for the same.
	function mark(node CsrIndex, seedIndex CsrIndex)
		try self.marked.set(node, value: true) otherwise panic("SubstrateFixpoint.mark: marked.set({node}) outside the node space")
		try self.seedOf.set(node, value: seedIndex) otherwise panic("SubstrateFixpoint.mark: seedOf.set({node}) outside the node space")
		self.worklist.push(node)
	end 'mark'

	// ⭐ THE FIXPOINT: every stdlib function that REACHES a seed over stdlib's own call edges inherits its
	// entry. A reverse BFS over the CSR adjacency — each node enters the worklist once (the marking IS the
	// visited test) and each edge is walked once, for O(nodes + edges).
	function propagateToCallers()
		let callersOf = buildCsr(self.edgeCallee, to: self.edgeCaller, nodeCount: self.marked.count())
		var head = 0 as CsrIndex
		while head < self.worklist.count() 'drain'
			let node = try self.worklist.get(head) otherwise panic("SubstrateFixpoint.propagateToCallers: worklist.get({head}) below its own count()")
			head = head + 1
			let seedIndex = try self.seedOf.get(node) otherwise panic("SubstrateFixpoint.propagateToCallers: seedOf.get({node}) outside the node space")
			for k in 0 upto callersOf.degree(node) 'eachCaller'
				let caller = callersOf.neighbour(node, k: k)
				if not (try self.marked.get(caller) otherwise panic("SubstrateFixpoint.propagateToCallers: marked.get({caller}) outside the node space")) 'newlyRequired'
					self.mark(caller, seedIndex: seedIndex)
				end 'newlyRequired'
			end 'eachCaller'
		end 'drain'
	end 'propagateToCallers'

	function requiresSubstrate(node CsrIndex) returns bool
		return try self.marked.get(node) otherwise panic("SubstrateFixpoint.requiresSubstrate: marked.get({node}) outside the node space")
	end 'requiresSubstrate'

	// The runtime entry `node` reaches. Defined only for a node `requiresSubstrate` accepts — an unmarked
	// node's `seedOf` slot was never written, and the panics say so rather than returning slot 0's name.
	function entryOf(node CsrIndex) returns ByteArray
		if not self.requiresSubstrate(node) 'unmarked'
			panic("SubstrateFixpoint.entryOf: node {node} needs no substrate")
		end 'unmarked'
		let seedIndex = try self.seedOf.get(node) otherwise panic("SubstrateFixpoint.entryOf: seedOf.get({node}) outside the node space")
		return try self.seedEntries.get(seedIndex) otherwise panic("SubstrateFixpoint.entryOf: node {node} names seed {seedIndex}, which does not exist")
	end 'entryOf'
end 'SubstrateFixpoint'

// One stdlib body op's contribution to the substrate fixpoint: it either NAMES a substrate runtime entry
// (a seed) or calls another stdlib function (a reverse edge). A callee can never be both — the parser
// reserves the `__` prefix for compiler-owned names, so no stdlib declaration can carry one.
//
// The band structure — and the band-append protection — is `markMaxonCalleeEdge`'s, the third member of
// that family in this file. Each asks a different question of the SAME four callee-naming variants, and
// each pays a different cost (this one runs only for a compile that calls into stdlib), which is why they
// are separate matches; a variant appended past `witnessTryDispatch` fails to compile in all three rather
// than being silently swallowed by any of them.
function noteSubstrateEdge(op MaxonOp, callerNode CsrIndex, nodeOf StdlibNodeByName, state SubstrateFixpoint)
	match op 'op'
		call(_, callee, _, _, _, _, _, _) then noteSubstrateCallee(callee, callerNode: callerNode, nodeOf: nodeOf, state: state)
		literal to stringLiteral then break 'op'
		tryCall(_, _, callee, _, _, _, _, _, _) then noteSubstrateCallee(callee, callerNode: callerNode, nodeOf: nodeOf, state: state)
		throwError to panicAbort then break 'op'
		functionRef(_, funcName) then noteSubstrateCallee(funcName, callerNode: callerNode, nodeOf: nodeOf, state: state)
		indirectCall then break 'op'
		asyncCall(_, callee, _, _, _, _, _) then noteSubstrateCallee(callee, callerNode: callerNode, nodeOf: nodeOf, state: state)
		await to witnessTryDispatch then break 'op'
		// A panic names no callee at all, so it reaches no stdlib substrate.
		panicAbortValue then break 'op'
	end 'op'
end 'noteSubstrateEdge'

// Classify ONE callee named by a stdlib body: a runtime entry that needs the Win32 substrate SEEDS the
// fixpoint, another stdlib function becomes a reverse edge, and anything else — a user callback, or a
// target-neutral runtime helper — carries no substrate requirement.
//
// ⭐ `calleeNeedsWin32Substrate` is asked, never restated: it is the ONE place that knows which runtime
// entries only x64-windows can provide, and a second list here would be that fact written down twice.
function noteSubstrateCallee(callee ByteArray, callerNode CsrIndex, nodeOf StdlibNodeByName, state SubstrateFixpoint)
	if calleeNeedsWin32Substrate(callee) 'substrateEntry'
		state.seed(callerNode, entry: callee)
		return
	end 'substrateEntry'

	if let calleeNode = try nodeOf.get(callee) 'stdlibSibling'
		state.callEdge(calleeNode, callerNode: callerNode)
	end 'stdlibSibling'
end 'noteSubstrateCallee'
